{"schema": "document.v2", "source_id": "eur-lex.europa.eu", "source_name": "EUR-Lex", "url": "https://eur-lex.europa.eu/browse/institutions/bank.html", "canonical_url": "https://eur-lex.europa.eu/browse/institutions/bank.html", "fetch_time": "2026-06-01T15:52:25.519562+00:00", "language": "en", "title": "European Central Bank", "published_date": "2026-06-01T15:52:23.992101+00:00", "updated_date": null, "doc_type": "Blog/News", "programme": ["Other/NA"], "finance_instrument": [], "stage": null, "actors": [], "tech_area": [], "monetary_values": [], "summary_150w": "Do you want to help improving EUR-Lex ? This is a list of experimental features that you can enable. These features are still under development; they are not fully tested, and might reduce EUR-Lex stability. Don't forget to give your feedback! This document is an excerpt from the EUR-Lex website\nEU law\nEU case-law\nOfficial Journal\nNational law and case-law\nInformation\nThe European Central Bank (ECB) is an EU institution operating under clearly defined rules which can be found in primary and secondary EU law. In carrying out its tasks, the ECB can adopt legal acts and also provide and publish opinions on proposed legislation within its fields of competence. In this section you will find all ECB legal acts – including amendments, corrigenda and consolidated versions – in all relevant official EU languages, as well as case law of relevance to the ECB. To learn more about the ECB, visit the ECB website ​ ​ . Please enable Javascript to display the statistics charts.\n\nReferences\n• Source — 01 Jun 2026 — https://eur-lex.europa.eu/browse/institutions/bank.html", "key_points": [], "implications": {"innovation_direction": [], "capital_structure": [], "regulatory_change": []}, "links": {"pdf": [], "dataset": [], "related": []}, "celex_id": null, "call_id": null, "award_id": null, "tags": [], "dedupe_signature": "af222e1b8acb32087dfa37525b75afc0ca3024bcb4351b161da73e0c3425e305", "embeddings": null, "extractio